		<h1 class="page-h1">How to Pace<br>a Backyard Ultra</h1>
		<p class="page-lede">We looked at 26 elite runners across five races to find the ideal loop pace. The answer might surprise you.</p>
	</header>

	<div class="page-section">
		<h2>The question</h2>
		<p>Standing at the finish arch at the BPN Go One More Backyard Ultra this past April, you'd see two completely different races playing out. Some runners came through the timing mat 10, even 12 minutes before the hour and walked back to camp. Others cut it fine. Mark Dowdle, one of the most experienced athletes in the sport, came in with barely a minute left more than once.</p>
		<p>Both approaches looked deliberate. Which one was right?</p>
		<p>We pulled lap-by-lap splits for the top five finishers from five major races to find out.</p>
	</div>

	<div class="page-section">
		<h2>The ideal pace window</h2>
		<p>We looked at lap splits for the top five finishers from five races: Big's Backyard Ultra 2023 and 2025, the Legends Backyard Ultra 2025 and 2026, and the BPN Go One More 2026. That's 26 athletes and thousands of loops of data.</p>
		<p>After removing sleep sprints and anomalous slow laps that skew the numbers, the pace across all 26 runners falls between <strong>47:45 and 51:48</strong> per loop. The overall median is <strong>49:55</strong>.</p>
		<p>If you're finishing loops around 50 minutes with eight or nine minutes to spare, you're not running too slowly. That buffer is the pace working as intended.</p>
