	<div class="page-section">
		<h2>What this means for you</h2>
		<p>The clearest lesson from all this is also the most counterintuitive: if you're finishing loops with eight or nine minutes to spare, that's probably not a training problem. The buffer is there for a reason. It's recovery time. Compress it into faster splits and you're borrowing against the next loop.</p>
		<p>One runner at a backyard ultra shared something after finishing 60-plus loops that stuck with us. His main takeaway was simple: stop watching other runners. Someone coming through the arch 10 minutes faster than you doesn't mean anything. Every loop starts at the same line. You're not racing them. You're racing the clock, and the clock is the same for everyone.</p>
		<p>Know your course, find your pace, and hold it. If the tent feels rushed with nine minutes on the clock, that's a tent problem. The data says the pace is probably fine.</p>
	</div>

